     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day. The usual maximum for adults is about 2,000\u20132,550 mg per day, and 2,550 mg is the labeled limit for immediate-release tablets. At 5,100 mg per day the dose is double that limit. That raises the risk of serious harm, including lactic acidosis. A single 1700 mg dose is also well above typical doses of 500\u20131000 mg. It may be a typo, for example 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg per day) or 1000 mg twice daily.\n\n2. **You're not a prescriber.** Medication orders should come from a licensed clinician who is responsible for the patient, or be verified by one.\n\nPlease ask the treating prescriber to confirm the intended dose and frequency. If they give me a corrected order, I can enter it, for example metformin 850 mg orally three times daily with meals. If they really do intend the original dose, they should enter it themselves and document the rationale."
      }
     ],
